I was wrong on both points and I withdraw them. The April 13, 2026 fee lett=
er cites ORS 192.345(40), and it is Exhibit 08 to my own petition. I should=
 have found it before I wrote. And ORS 192.329(4)(a) makes good faith the c=
ondition for suspending the processing
 deadlines, so the order's reference to it was necessary rather than gratui=
tous. My letter of this morning was mistaken in both respects and the corre=
ction is published in my archive alongside it.</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
<br>
</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
What the fee letter does not do is answer the question I have been asking s=
ince April 3.</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
<br>
</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
The letter identifies the records the Department located. That list has one=
 entry: &quot;CAD =97 $12.50.&quot; The body camera sentence is not in that=
 list. It reads: &quot;The body cam is exempt from disclosure. ORS 192.345(=
40) exempts audio or video recordings resulting from
 a law enforcement officer's operation of a video camera worn upon the offi=
cer's person=85&quot; That is the text of the statute. It does not state th=
at a body-worn camera recording of the March 24, 2026 contact exists. It do=
es not say how many, or when, or which
 officer wore the camera.</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
<br>
</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
ORS 192.329(2)(b) requires a public body to assert the exemptions it believ=
es apply to requested records. An assertion that identifies no record is a =
statement of law, not a determination about a record. On April 3 the Depart=
ment told me a search &quot;identified
 no records responsive to your request.&quot; Ten days later it identified =
one, the CAD, and quoted a statute about a category of recording. I still d=
o not know whether a recording exists.</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
<br>
</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
Three things follow, and I ask the Department to address each.</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
<br>
</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
ORS 192.345(40) is conditional. Recordings within it are exempt &quot;unles=
s the public interest requires disclosure in the particular instance.&quot;=
 A one-sentence recitation of the statute in a fee letter reflects no weigh=
ing of the public interest in this instance,
 and none could have been performed on a record the letter does not identif=
y.</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
<br>
</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
The exemption assertion carried no statement of review rights. ORS 192.329(=
2)(f) requires one whenever a body asserts an exemption. The April 13 lette=
r contains payment instructions and a mailing address. It contains no notic=
e that I could seek review, which
 is the notice that would have told me an exemption had been asserted at al=
l.</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
<br>
</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
The July 14, 2026 release letter says the opposite. &quot;The document we a=
re releasing has been reviewed for exempt material, with none being identif=
ied.&quot; If a body-worn camera recording had been located and withheld un=
der ORS 192.345(40), that sentence cannot
 be accurate. If no recording was ever located, the April 13 sentence descr=
ibed nothing. I ask the Department to state which is the case.</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
nt,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size:12pt; color:=
rgb(0,0,0)">
<br>
</div>
<div class=3D"x_elementToProof" style=3D"font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFo=
